<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gurung</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tamu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gurung, who call themselves Tamu, are a people of the hills of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, especially around the Annapurna range in Lamjung, Kaski and Gorkha. Speaking a Tibeto-Burman language and following Buddhist, shamanic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> practices, they served as soldiers in the armies of the Gorkha kingdom and later, in large numbers, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkhas</a> in the British and Indian armies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gurung.jpg" alt="Gurung" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SPakhrin · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gurung_Tamu_Lhosar_2018_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Gurung call themselves Tamu, and Gurung is the name used by outsiders. According to oral tradition, Gurung derives from a Tibetan word, gru-gu, meaning to bring down, which reflects a migration from the Tibetan plateau to the southern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>. The origin of the people has been traced back to the Qiang people of Qinghai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Gurung have historically lived a semi-nomadic life, herding sheep and yaks in the Himalayan foothills, although many have since taken up other occupations.</p><p>They live mostly in the hills and mountains of Gandaki Province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2011, 798,658 people, 2.97 per cent of the population of Nepal, identified as Gurung. They form a large share of the people of Manang, Lamjung, Kaski, Mustang and Gorkha districts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> ended and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Treaty of Sugauli</a> was signed in 1816, the British began to recruit soldiers for the British Army from the northern villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. Most came from four peoples, one of which was the Gurung. Gurungs served in the Gorkha regiments of the British and Indian armies and in the Singapore Police Force, and they played a prominent part in both world wars. Their service brought decorations, among them Victoria Crosses. Gurungs are still recruited into the British, Indian and Bruneian armies, and they retire after serving between 15 and 35 years. In 1999 the British government changed its policy and allowed Gurkha retirees of the British Army to settle in the United Kingdom with their families.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Gurung language, also called Tamu Kyi, is a tonal Tibeto-Burman language. It was traditionally passed on by word of mouth and is now also written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanagari">Devanagari</a> and in community-developed scripts. Most speakers are also fluent in Nepali, and migration and urbanisation have reduced its use among younger people. The main festival is Tamu Lhosar, which marks the new year on the 15th of Poush, in December or January.</p><p>Gurung religion includes the traditions called Ghyabri and Pachyu. Lamas perform Buddhist rites at births, funerals and Lhosar, mainly in Lamjung, Parbat, Kaski, Manang and Mustang, while some villages keep remnants of the pre-Buddhist Bon religion and of an older shamanic belief system. Traditional dress was made of handwoven wool and plant fibres such as nettle. Women wear the cholo blouse, the phariya skirt and the patuki sash, with ornaments; men wear the kachhad, a white wrap, with the bhoto shirt. It is now worn mainly at festivals and rituals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gurung people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gorkha was a poor but well-organised state whose kings relied on a militarised peasantry of Khas, Magar and <b>Gurung</b> soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Lamjung was a small agricultural state of terraced fields with a <b>Gurung</b> population and a Hindu ruling family.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lamjung">Kingdom of Lamjung</a></li></ul></section>
